WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.890
Ms Salma's mark sheet

2
00:00:01.890 --> 00:00:03.290
looks perfect on paper.

3
00:00:03.950 --> 00:00:06.090
But when I asked the computer for the

4
00:00:06.090 --> 00:00:08.190
average, it gave me an error.

5
00:00:08.830 --> 00:00:11.810
Merged cells, a Total row, and

6
00:00:11.810 --> 00:00:13.590
two marks in one cell!

7
00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:18.080
Today: sheets a computer can use, and a

8
00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:19.940
first look at Python reading them.

9
00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:24.280
A computer reads a sheet very literally.

10
00:00:25.070 --> 00:00:26.970
It needs one header row,

11
00:00:26.970 --> 00:00:29.090
then one row per pupil.

12
00:00:29.780 --> 00:00:33.160
One fact per column, one value per cell.

13
00:00:33.790 --> 00:00:36.250
No merged cells, no blank rows,

14
00:00:36.630 --> 00:00:38.290
and no totals in between.

15
00:00:39.580 --> 00:00:40.520
Keep two sheets.

16
00:00:41.370 --> 00:00:43.730
The raw data, exactly as collected,

17
00:00:44.190 --> 00:00:45.570
which you never type over.

18
00:00:46.130 --> 00:00:48.970
And a summary sheet, where formulas like

19
00:00:48.970 --> 00:00:52.850
AVERAGE and COUNTIF work on the tidy data.

20
00:00:54.090 --> 00:00:56.090
Filters and sorting work too,

21
00:00:56.390 --> 00:00:57.530
once the sheet is tidy.

22
00:00:58.120 --> 00:00:59.880
Show only Six B.

23
00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:02.820
Sort by maths mark, highest first.

24
00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:05.880
And because every row is complete,

25
00:01:06.460 --> 00:01:07.720
nothing gets left behind.

26
00:01:09.020 --> 00:01:11.940
Save the tidy sheet as CSV, and a

27
00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:13.120
program can read it too.

28
00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:16.740
In Python, import csv, open the

29
00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:18.720
file, and read it row by row.

30
00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:22.460
Each row arrives as a dictionary: you

31
00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:24.020
ask for a column by its name.

32
00:01:25.210 --> 00:01:27.250
To add the marks, turn the text

33
00:01:27.250 --> 00:01:28.970
into numbers with int.

34
00:01:29.650 --> 00:01:31.310
Keep a running total, and

35
00:01:31.310 --> 00:01:32.950
count the rows as you go.

36
00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:35.540
Then divide: that's the average.

37
00:01:36.890 --> 00:01:37.470
Quick check!

38
00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:40.740
My sheet had a Total row

39
00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:41.920
right under the pupils.

40
00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:44.940
Why did the computer's average

41
00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:47.040
come out far too high?

42
00:01:48.250 --> 00:01:50.730
The Total row counted as one more

43
00:01:50.730 --> 00:01:52.870
pupil, with a huge mark.

44
00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:54.980
Keep totals on the summary

45
00:01:54.980 --> 00:01:57.160
sheet, never inside the data.

46
00:01:57.800 --> 00:02:00.400
Then the average is honest, whether a

47
00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:02.440
person or a program works it out.

48
00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:05.480
In the app, Mr Bello's sheet

49
00:02:05.480 --> 00:02:07.360
answers three questions wrong.

50
00:02:08.010 --> 00:02:10.730
Make one fix at a time, and read the

51
00:02:10.730 --> 00:02:12.530
reason under each wrong answer.

52
00:02:13.180 --> 00:02:14.800
Notice when the marks column

53
00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:16.880
turns from text into numbers.

54
00:02:18.220 --> 00:02:18.980
Your turn.

55
00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:21.960
Python reads every value in

56
00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:23.520
a CSV file as text.

57
00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:26.940
What must I do before I add up the marks?

58
00:02:28.220 --> 00:02:29.660
Turn each value into a

59
00:02:29.660 --> 00:02:32.160
number with int, then add.

60
00:02:32.790 --> 00:02:36.170
Remember: one row per thing, one value

61
00:02:36.170 --> 00:02:38.810
per cell, and no totals inside.

62
00:02:39.580 --> 00:02:41.600
Keep the raw data, and

63
00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:43.320
summarise on another sheet.

64
00:02:43.940 --> 00:02:46.440
And a tidy CSV can be read by

65
00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:48.160
people and programs alike.

66
00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:51.940
Try it in class tomorrow: give groups a

67
00:02:51.940 --> 00:02:54.200
messy paper mark sheet you made up, with

68
00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:57.520
merged cells, totals and two marks in a

69
00:02:57.520 --> 00:02:57.700
cell.

70
00:02:58.250 --> 00:03:00.510
They redraw it as a tidy table.

71
00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:03.760
Then compare: whose table gives

72
00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:05.180
the right class average?
